  "chunk": "terns fail or training data falls short. For example, it does not take a human\n—even an infant— an excessive amount of training data (e.g., pictures of a cat)\nto be able to recognize a cat, to generalize the concept of a cat, and subsequently\nbe able to reliable distinguish a cat from other objects (e.g., a rabbit).\nThis raises questions about the extent to which LLMs can demonstrate gen-\nuine understanding and problem-solving capabilities. Text-based games provide\na testbed for investigating this question as there is little to no room for deploy-\ning ‘tricks’ to genuinely understand the game’s mechanics as there is only sparse\nfeedback. Rather, human players should leverage imagination, world knowledge,\nand adaptive reasoning to understand these fictional environments [25].\nZork, originally developed at MIT and released in 1977 (later commercially\nreleased by Infocom [13] in 1980), represents a canonical example of interac-",
